Both flash parts protect areas of the chip by status register 1's BP0
to BP4 and status register 2's CMP, by one table (HARDWARE-SPEC HW-121,
HW-131), and ignore an erase or program whose range holds a protected
byte. Upstream's chip read status 2 as 0 and status 1 as only its busy
and write-enable bits, and carried out every erase and program, so no
test could make a firmware's read-back of an erase fail (clean's #193).
With the patch the chip keeps both status registers' other bits as
given at power-up (`status`), reads them back by 0x05 and 0x35, and
ignores a protected erase or program wholly: nothing changes, it isn't
logged or counted as an operation (so power-cut counts are as before),
the chip doesn't go busy, and the write-enable bit is reset. The
P25Q80H's datasheet says that reset for its 32 KiB erase alone; the
patch applies it to every ignored erase and program. Neither datasheet
says whether the busy bit rises. A chip erase goes ahead only when nothing is
protected. The status register writes (0x01, 0x31) are not modelled.
The crate tests each_protection_setting_covers_its_rows_area and
a_protected_erase_or_program_is_ignored fail without it.

